### `src/parser/unitReference.ts` (new file)
**Before:** Didn't exist. There was no way to convert local transport-unit language ("gunia," "mkokoteni," "fuso") into kilograms.

**After:** Contains:
- `UNIT_KG_BASELINE` — the direct kg-per-unit lookup table, taken from your reference data:

  | Unit | kg |
  |---|---|
  | gunia (sack) | 30 |
  | toroli (wheelbarrow) | 40 |
  | mkokoteni (handcart) | 250 |
  | mtumbwi (small boat) | 500 |
  | sese (large boat) | 1500 |
  | pickup | 800 |
  | canter (light truck) | 2500 |
  | fuso (large truck) | 6000 |

- `UNIT_ALIASES` — maps the many Sheng/Swahili/English spellings and synonyms (e.g. "magunia," "sitoro," "boti ndogo," "lorry kubwa") to one canonical `LocalUnit` key.
- `estimateKgFromUnit(unit, count)` — pure function: `UNIT_KG_BASELINE[unit] * count`.
- `resolveUnitAlias(term)` — normalizes a raw matched word/phrase to a `LocalUnit`, or `null` if unrecognized.

**Why:** This is the "40kg/m² arithmetic" requirement, but implemented as a direct kg lookup (informed by, and consistent with, the fuller volumetric/area reference table you provided) rather than recomputing area × density every time. A lookup table is less error-prone and easier to adjust as real-world reference sizes get refined. Isolating it in its own file means the LLM never touches this math — the LLM only ever says "1 mtumbwi," and this file is solely responsible for turning that into 500kg.

